WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.200
Peaceable assembly is the cornerstone

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.990
to the ongoing fight for equal rights.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.590
And in America, we look towards
the Civil Rights Movement

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:15.530
of the 1950s and '60s
as our primary model.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:17.740
"It all started when Mrs. Rosa Parks

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:21.120
wouldn't give up her seat
on the bus to a white man."

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:22.179
"This is rehearsal,

00:00:22.179 --> 00:00:25.200
man getting to know their machines."

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:26.760
But at the dawn of World War II,

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.840
over twenty years before the
famous march on Washington,

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.728
a hundred thousand Black
activists came within ten days

00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:35.040
of marching on the Capitol,

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.867
forcing a crucial early inflection point

00:00:36.867 --> 00:00:39.023
in the fight for civil rights.

00:00:39.890 --> 00:00:43.040
"The march at Washington
in 1941 was a precursor

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.010
to the more famous march in 1963.

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:51.480
And although the one in
1941 did not actually happen

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.530
it was successful in
achieving its objective."

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:56.130
So how do you quickly mobilize

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.240
a hundred thousand Americans in a time

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.470
long before social media
or even television?

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:04.440
This is the story of the
March that Almost Happened,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:05.693
and the man behind it.

00:01:11.170 --> 00:01:13.540
Though the Civil Rights Act of 1875

00:01:13.540 --> 00:01:15.616
technically guaranteed equal rights,

00:01:15.616 --> 00:01:18.990
lax enforcement, various
Supreme Court Rulings,

00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.110
intimidation, segregation, and violence

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.810
systematically ensured
that people of color

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:27.263
remained left out of the American Dream.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:31.160
It would take almost another century

00:01:31.160 --> 00:01:34.038
to put an end to segregation
and begin the long process

00:01:34.038 --> 00:01:36.010
of equal rights.

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:38.120
But icons who led the
Civil Rights Movement,

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:40.270
like Rosa Parks, and Martin Luther King,

00:01:40.270 --> 00:01:42.240
didn't exist in a vacuum.

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.240
There are a lot of
players who didn't wind up

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.863
becoming household names.

00:01:51.260 --> 00:01:53.270
"Although the Civil Rights Movement that

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.330
we're most familiar with begins in 1955

00:01:56.330 --> 00:01:58.280
with the Montgomery Bus boycott,

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:00.930
it had roots at a much earlier period.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:04.390
In many ways the
strategies, and the tactics,

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:08.020
and some of the people
involved were the same."

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:09.780
Enter A. Philip Randolph.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.086
"We want complete equality.

00:02:12.086 --> 00:02:15.430
Social, economic, and political.

00:02:15.430 --> 00:02:20.430
And no force under the
sun, can stand, and block,

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.760
and stop this Civil Rights Revolution."

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.050
"Although Randolph is
probably less well known

00:02:27.050 --> 00:02:30.670
to most people today, he was at the time,

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.550
one of the most prominent

00:02:32.550 --> 00:02:35.870
and respected leaders
in the Black Movement.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:37.835
"Roy Wilkins with A. Philip Randolph,

00:02:37.835 --> 00:02:40.693
they have fought their fight
all of their adult lives."

00:02:42.430 --> 00:02:45.420
Born in 1889, Randolph
was raised in Florida,

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.620
migrating up to New York
City as a young adult

00:02:47.620 --> 00:02:50.390
to study Economics and Philosophy.

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:53.230
"He was drawn to the, to socialism,

00:02:53.230 --> 00:02:54.980
and to the Socialist Party.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:02:57.770
He started a newspaper
called The Messenger.

00:02:57.770 --> 00:03:01.130
It was a very much working
class oriented publication."

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.380
The Messenger advocated for Black unions

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.260
resisted the draft in World War I,

00:03:05.260 --> 00:03:08.980
and pushed for integration
and equality across the board.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:10.920
As a major promoter of Black artists,

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.720
The Messenger is also credited for helping

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.200
launch an explosion of
music, art and literature

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:17.030
that began in the 1920s,

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:18.870
known today as the Harlem Renaissance.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:28.090
Randolph's high profile
from The Messenger,

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.460
plus his reputation as a Union organizer,

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:32.619
caught the attention of 500 overworked,

00:03:32.619 --> 00:03:35.310
underpaid railroad workers.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:38.040
In 1925, a secret meeting in Harlem,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.310
established the first all Black union:

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.200
The Brotherhood of the
Sleeping Car Porters.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.390
"The Brotherhood of Sleeping Car Porters

00:03:47.390 --> 00:03:50.630
is a union of railway porters,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:53.710
but its roots go back to
the end of the Civil War.

00:03:53.710 --> 00:03:57.170
A man named George Pullman,
organized a company

00:03:57.170 --> 00:04:00.250
to provide sleeper cars
for railway travel.

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:02.283
It's sort of similar to

00:04:02.283 --> 00:04:05.430
First Class travel today on airlines.

00:04:05.430 --> 00:04:06.750
Except in this case, of course,

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.220
he drew on a Black
labor force exclusively,

00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:13.290
who would serve people
food, clean up after them,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:14.283
make their bed.

00:04:14.283 --> 00:04:16.890
They weren't even accorded the dignity

00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.940
of having their own names.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.340
They were all called George."

00:04:22.330 --> 00:04:24.430
Despite the demeaning nature of the work,

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:26.140
this job offered one of the only

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.780
upwardly mobile opportunities
for Black Americans

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.120
who migrated to the
north hoping the escape

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:32.233
the Jim Crow south.

00:04:33.090 --> 00:04:37.160
"It was an occupation
of some respectability.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:39.059
I mean it was after all, in this period,

00:04:39.059 --> 00:04:43.360
when most Black laborers were
on plantations and farms,

00:04:43.360 --> 00:04:44.860
and so they were looked up to."

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.660
While White travelers let these porters

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:53.310
shine their shoes, make their
beds, and serve them food,

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:57.600
the Brotherhood quietly,
and methodically organized,

00:04:57.600 --> 00:04:59.760
using the motto, "Fight or be Slaves",

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.270
Randolph led an expansion of
the union to over 18 thousand

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:04.870
members nationwide.

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:07.890
And ironically, a key to
the Brotherhood's success

00:05:07.890 --> 00:05:09.790
was the very system the relegated them

00:05:09.790 --> 00:05:11.890
to their servile cast:

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:12.723
the railroad.

00:05:14.040 --> 00:05:18.409
"The very nature of the
work enabled them to develop

00:05:18.409 --> 00:05:20.990
strong connections among themselves,

00:05:20.990 --> 00:05:23.324
pride and a militancy that won them

00:05:23.324 --> 00:05:27.920
considerable approval in the
Black community as a whole.

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:30.270
A porter that gets on a train in New York,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:35.000
and he travels to Atlanta,
and maybe down to Florida,

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.060
or Louisiana up to
Chicago, or to California,

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.150
and in the process
they're making connections

00:05:41.150 --> 00:05:44.090
among communities that
otherwise might be isolated.

00:05:44.090 --> 00:05:46.610
Black newspapers, like
the Chicago Defender

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:49.270
would use porters to
distribute their papers.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:51.560
They'd become the
instrument of communication,

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:54.070
nationwide people would get information

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:57.010
about what was going on in
Chicago, in Mississippi,

00:05:57.010 --> 00:05:58.100
and vice versa.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:06:00.630
And the porters were
instrumental in creating

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.081
that sense of community
and of connection."

00:06:03.081 --> 00:06:05.600
This was their social network,

00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.440
the powerful potential of
which Randolph cultivated

00:06:08.440 --> 00:06:11.880
to push for better working
conditions and higher pay.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.370
But up to this point,
Randolph had not yet been able

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:16.870
to leverage this power
beyond union organizing

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:18.540
and collective bargaining.

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:21.033
But that would all change
after Pearl Harbor.

00:06:25.040 --> 00:06:30.040
"December 7, 1941 a date
which will live in infamy."

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:38.990
As FDR's war mobilization

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:41.450
began to lift the nation
out of the Great Depression,

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.320
African Americans were
left out of the boom.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.580
"Black Americans were
excluded, by and large,

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.717
from fairly lucrative jobs in many cases,

00:06:50.717 --> 00:06:53.490
in these defense industries.

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:58.490
And Randolph, as a union
leader, raised a protest

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:02.740
to the government that
if they did not deal

00:07:02.740 --> 00:07:05.340
with this problem, that is
the racial discrimination

00:07:05.340 --> 00:07:06.840
against Black workers,

00:07:06.840 --> 00:07:09.903
that he would bring a
march on Washington."

00:07:11.560 --> 00:07:12.640
And as it turns out,

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:14.900
Randolph had a supporter
in the White House,

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:16.990
the First Lady, who
took a greater interest

00:07:16.990 --> 00:07:19.290
in Civil Rights issues than her husband.

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:22.490
In his 1941 correspondence
with Eleanor Roosevelt,

00:07:22.490 --> 00:07:24.330
Randolph put the White House on notice

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:26.850
of his plans to organize
a march on Washington

00:07:26.850 --> 00:07:29.230
to advocate for the right to work.

00:07:29.230 --> 00:07:31.530
But President Roosevelt's
priority was to mobilize

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.550
the war effort as quickly
and smoothly as possible.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:37.380
He rejected Randolph's demands outright.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:40.140
Undeterred, Randolph
continued to plan the march

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:43.920
and word spread through the
Brotherhood's massive network.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:45.850
With less than two weeks before the march,

00:07:45.850 --> 00:07:47.920
the First Lady helped
arrange a meeting between

00:07:47.920 --> 00:07:51.320
Randolph and President
Roosevelt in the Oval Office.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:53.930
It was not just him, it
was also Walter White,

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:56.733
who was the head of the NAACP,

00:07:56.733 --> 00:07:59.933
and Mary McLeod Bethune
who was a major leader

00:07:59.933 --> 00:08:02.860
among Black women's organizations.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:06.180
And Roosevelt seemed to want to avoid

00:08:06.180 --> 00:08:09.790
actually addressing the
demands that Randolph had made.

00:08:09.790 --> 00:08:11.930
Randolph at some point
interrupts him and says,

00:08:11.930 --> 00:08:15.230
you know, if we don't get
some kind of satisfaction

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:17.906
on our demands, we will
bring a hundred thousand

00:08:17.906 --> 00:08:21.280
Black protestors to Washington D.C.

00:08:21.280 --> 00:08:25.170
And Roosevelt's head snapped
back, and he was incredulous,

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:27.930
he turned to Walter White and asked him,

00:08:27.930 --> 00:08:29.181
Well, can he do that?

00:08:29.181 --> 00:08:32.550
Which White answers, Yep, he can do that.

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:35.293
And the reason he could
do it was because of

00:08:35.293 --> 00:08:37.363
the Sleeping Car Porters."

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:43.800
President Roosevelt grudgingly signed

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:47.810
an executive order 8802,
ensuring fair hiring practices

00:08:47.810 --> 00:08:49.900
to Black Americans and establishing the

00:08:49.900 --> 00:08:51.790
Fair Employment Practices Commission,

00:08:51.790 --> 00:08:53.390
which was empowered to investigate,

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:56.130
and overturn race
discrimination in industries

00:08:56.130 --> 00:08:58.700
engaged in military production.

00:08:58.700 --> 00:09:00.550
This was a major early victory

00:09:00.550 --> 00:09:01.920
for the Civil Rights Movement.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.027
Made possible by A.
Philip Randolph's ability

00:09:04.027 --> 00:09:06.490
to quickly mobilize Black Americans

00:09:06.490 --> 00:09:08.160
in a time where the closest thing we had

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.020
to mass communication
was FDR's Fireside Chats.

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:15.710
"Nobody knows when total
victory would come."

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.120
But Randolph did have to make some

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:19.560
concessions, whose
original demands included

00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:22.370
the desegregation of the armed forces.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:24.860
For Roosevelt, that was a bridge too far,

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:27.666
who feared alienating military
brass just before the war

00:09:27.666 --> 00:09:31.093
and going head to head with
southern democrats in Congress.

00:09:33.430 --> 00:09:35.880
Randolph continued his
fight after the war,

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:37.540
organizing protests outside

00:09:37.540 --> 00:09:40.400
the Democratic National
Convention in 1948.

00:09:40.400 --> 00:09:42.930
And shortly after Truman won the election,

00:09:42.930 --> 00:09:45.660
he signed executive order 9981,

00:09:45.660 --> 00:09:48.030
desegregating the armed forces.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:51.040
"We look at the March in Washington in 41

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:52.485
that didn't happen,

00:09:52.485 --> 00:09:54.860
the movement continued.

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.250
You know, that you don't
win it in a single battle,

00:09:57.250 --> 00:09:59.343
it is a long drawn out struggle."

00:10:04.920 --> 00:10:07.030
Two decades later, A. Philip Randolph

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:09.270
would finally get his march on Washington.

00:10:09.270 --> 00:10:11.710
Playing the lead role in
organizing the largest

00:10:11.710 --> 00:10:14.350
Civil Rights Demonstration
in American History.

00:10:14.350 --> 00:10:17.300
"Today the city of
Washington takes notice.

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:21.440
Our leaders, A. Philip Randolph,
Roy Wilkins, Reverend King

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.510
receive the keys to the city."

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:26.470
Then the following year, this happened.

00:10:26.470 --> 00:10:31.340
"I'm about to sign into law,
the Civil Rights Act of 1964"

00:10:32.228 --> 00:10:33.890
"It's fair to say that

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:35.900
without the march that would have been

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:37.810
unlikely that there would
have been much progress

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.060
passing that legislation."

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:42.700
A. Philip Randolph's fingerprint

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:45.440
on the Civil Rights
Movement remains indelible.

00:10:45.440 --> 00:10:48.910
His persistence, strategies,
and tactics are timeless.

00:10:48.910 --> 00:10:51.270
And are studied by
anyone hoping to organize

00:10:51.270 --> 00:10:52.886
for change today.

00:10:52.886 --> 00:10:56.803
"Peaceful protests! Peaceful protests!"

